king at this AE equal a plus  one they're basically the same thing  it's just a really quick and easy way of  writing it so that you don't have to  write the full statement now you can do  this for a lot of the arithmetic  operators so you can do that for  subtraction which is really equal to a  minus one you can do it for  multiplication which is equivalent to a  times two and you can do it for division  which is equal to a divided by two and  finally there is the modulo which is  equivalent to a a13 I'll cover what  modulo does in a minute but the point  here is that you've got these assignment  operators and what they do is they  assign the value from the right side to  the left side now let's take a look at  the arithmetic operators  and these are comprised of mainly the  standard arithmetic operators that you  might be familiar with from other  programming languages such as addition  subtraction multiplication division and  division with remainder now I'll work  with numbers here just so you can see  the values so beginning with addition a  plus 1 you can guess that if a is 10  then this will be equal to 11 a minus 1  if a is 10 then this will be equal to 9  and a multiplied by 2 if a is 10 then  this will be equal to 20 and if a is 10  and a divided by 2 then this will be  equal to 5 now the only slightly tricky  one here is the modulo or the division  with remainder what this does is it does  the division but instead of giving you  the result there it gives you the  remaining value to give an example I'll  use real numbers here so 10 divided by 3  that is equal to 3 with a remainder with  a remainder of 1 so 10 mod 3 is equal to  1 now 10 divided by 3 is actually 3  points for you but we're just going to  use indicator division if you now know  and you can see that modulo is the  remainder after division integer  division  now this modulo operator might seem a  little bit of special case but it's  actually really really important and  commonly used often I use it when I'm  iterating lists for example we'll get to  it later when we start talking about lit  for loops and that kind of thing  especially when you get to for loops and  arrays for now understand how it works  we'll be covering use cases quite a bit  later on now there's a couple more  arithmetic operators that you may see  and remember where I talked about unary  versus binary operators all the ones  that we've covered so far have been  binary operators but these next three or  all unary operators meaning they operate  only on a single operand so a plus plus  or plus plus a this is called the  increment operator and depending on how  you use it it will either increment the  value and then return it or return the  value and then increment it afterwards  so imagine that a equals one and you go  let B equals a plus plus in that case  since the plus plus comes after or it's  a what's called postfix then it will  return the value before incrementing so  B should be equal to one but then a will  be equal to two once this runs now have  you do the same thing now if you do the  same thing but the plus plus is the  prefix then it will increment a before  returning the value meaning B will be  two and a will be to the next arithmetic  operator is the decrement operator so a  minus minus or minus minus minus minus a  and that operates exactly the same way  as the plus plus except it decrements  instead of increments and we're not  going to go through an example because  we just ran through it for plus plus  let's give these names here  and finally the last arithmetic operator  that we're going to cover today this  isn't all of them but this is good  enough this is the negation and what it  does is it negates the value so if you  can't see that that's a minus sign and  if you were to go something like that B  equals negative a all you have to do is  add the minus symbol and it negates the  value it's equivalent to multiplying by  negative one now I like I said this  isn't technically all of the earth  medical operators but it's most of them  and probably the ones that you'll need  in the near future and the last thing I  want to go over is the comparison  operators so let's go over these first  up is equals equals so what happens if  you go a equals equals B what this does  is it checks whether or not the two  operands are equal so a does not equal  to B as you might guess the checks  whether or not a does not equal B so  while a equal equals B would return true  if they were the same a does not equal B  would return true if they are not the  same now we have something called strict  equal and what this does is it's really  similar to equals equals except that the  extra equal sign indicates that you also  want to check whether or not the types  are exactly the same and just like  equals equals there is a strict not  equals then we get into some of the  other comparison ones so let's say that  you have a greater than B that checks  whether or not is a is greater than B so  if a is 10 and B is 5 then 10 is greater  than 5 and that's true for example is  less than  that's doesn't be this would be checking  whether or not a is smaller than B is  greater than or equal to B so this is  the greater than or equal to operator  and it just checks whether or not the  left operand is greater than or equal to  the right operand and you can imagine  that there is the exact opposite so a is  less than or equal to B and so for the  most part those are the different ways  to do comparisons this was pretty  information dense and I kind of sped  through a lot of these but there are a  lot of operators to cover and I didn't  want to drag this out for two or three  hours while we went into deep examples  and code for each one you should be able  to play with these and get to know them  there's quite a few operators here and  we only covered just a fraction of the  ones that exist in JavaScript but  there's a lot of repetition here as well  once you get to know one or a few of  them you kind of get to know the rest in  the series anyway that about wraps up  the different operators in JavaScript  you should have at least a passing  understanding now of how they work and  what the different operators are if you  have any questions or concerns or you  just want to follow up on something that  was said in the video let me know in the  comments below but that about wraps up  this video in the series I hope you  enjoyed it and I'll see you soon  Cheers
 

